body.zh:
只展示会影响当下选择的字段：

> **当前任务**：{人物可执行的目标}
>
> **完成条件**：{可观察的达成标准}
>
> **剩余限制**：{时间、次数或当前不可用条件}
>
> **成功所得**：{与主角目标有关的回报}
>
> **使用代价**：{现在就能理解的后果}
>
> **未解锁信息**：只在“未知”本身会改变策略时保留；否则删掉。

面板出现的判断标准是：读完后，主角是否要重新选择。若只是把读者已经知道的属性重复一遍，可以用一句人物反应代替整页列表。

body.en:
Display only fields that affect the present choice:

> **Current task:** {An objective the character can perform}
>
> **Completion condition:** {An observable standard}
>
> **Remaining limit:** {Time, uses, or a condition currently preventing use}
>
> **Reward:** {Something connected to the protagonist’s goal}
>
> **Cost of use:** {A consequence understandable now}
>
> **Locked information:** Keep this only when the unknown itself changes strategy; otherwise remove it.

Use this test: after reading the panel, must the protagonist choose again? If it merely repeats attributes readers already know, replace the full list with one character reaction.

While cuts, narration placement, and speaking duration are still changing, subtitle in and out points will change with them. If you fine-tune every cue too early, moving one line of narration later can leave mistimed, obstructed, or outdated text behind.

Before detailed layout, confirm that:

- the shot order and main cuts are set;
- the narration text matches the actual recording in use;
- you know which original dialogue remains and which hard subtitles are already embedded;
- the positions of signs, documents, and interfaces the audience must read are marked;
- the delivery canvas and any overlapping titles or corner labels have an actual plan.

When the spoken content changes, update the narration and recording before changing the corresponding subtitle. Editing only the on-screen text does not change what the audience actually hears.

A static sample frame confirms only one moment. During full playback, also check whether:

- subtitles appear before the voice begins or linger too long after it ends;
- an old subtitle remains after a cut into the next scene;
- a moving character, raised hand, or displayed object enters the subtitle region;
- the outline, shadow, or backing stays legible as the image shifts between bright and dark;
- a long line suddenly shrinks to fit, causing the type size to jump across the video;
- source hard subtitles, narration subtitles, and titles occupy the same region at once.

Fix the layer that contains the problem. If the text is too long, remove repeated information first. If the position obstructs the image, adjust the layout for that cue or group of cues. If the timing is wrong, check it against the actual sound. If hard subtitles are already embedded in the source pixels, follow the approved editing plan for the picture or choose another region. Do not use animation or borders to conceal incorrect timing.

Judge reading allowance, sound-leading cuts, and speech across shots against this project’s choices. Do not impose identical speech and cue boundaries on every work. A wording or recording change after layout requires updated text, audio, and timing evidence, followed by review of the new export.
